title: Kaseya VSA / REvil
page: breaches/kaseya-vsa-revil.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: On July 2, 2021, REvil affiliates exploited zero-days in on-premises Kaseya VSA servers and pushed ransomware through the product's own agent update mechanism, encrypting roughly 1,500 businesses via around 50 to 60 MSPs.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: ~1,500 | businesses encrypted | downstream, through around 50 to 60 compromised MSPs
stat: ~800 | Coop stores closed in Sweden | a downstream victim of a downstream victim
stat: $70M | demanded for a universal decryptor | by REvil; it was not paid
The vulnerabilities were already known to Kaseya: the Dutch Institute for Vulnerability Disclosure (DIVD) had reported them, and a patch was in development.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with.

On July 2, 2021, REvil affiliates exploited vulnerabilities in on-premises Kaseya VSA servers and used the product's own agent update mechanism to push ransomware downstream. Around 50 to 60 managed service providers, or MSPs, were compromised, and roughly 1,500 downstream businesses were encrypted. Keep the words around and roughly when you say these numbers.

Sweden's Coop closed some 800 supermarkets because its point-of-sale system was administered by an affected provider. The page calls Coop a downstream victim of a downstream victim.

REvil demanded $70 million for a universal decryptor. It was not paid.

Then the uncomfortable part. The vulnerabilities were already known to Kaseya: the Dutch Institute for Vulnerability Disclosure had reported them, and a patch was in development when the attack landed.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: Before the attack | DIVD has reported the flaws to Kaseya; a patch is in development
event: July 2, 2021 | REvil affiliates exploit VSA servers and push ransomware, timed for the July 4 weekend
event: July 11-12, 2021 | The patch is released, roughly ten days after the attack
event: Later in July, weeks after the attack | The FBI has obtained the key; Kaseya distributes a universal decryptor
The page gives no date for DIVD's report. REvil's infrastructure went offline days after the attack; the page gives no exact date. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives only a few dates, and the timeline shows only those.

Before the attack, the Dutch Institute for Vulnerability Disclosure, DIVD, had reported the flaws to Kaseya, and Kaseya was working on a patch. The page gives no date for the report, so do not supply one.

On July 2, 2021, the Friday before the US Independence Day weekend, REvil affiliates exploited on-premises VSA servers and pushed ransomware through the agent update mechanism. The page says the timing was chosen deliberately, when staffing was thinnest.

The patch was released on July 11-12, 2021, roughly ten days after the attack. Later in July, after the FBI obtained the decryption key, Kaseya distributed a universal decryptor to affected customers. The page says it arrived weeks later for those still waiting.

REvil's infrastructure went offline days after the attack, and the group was later disrupted. The page gives no date for either, so they sit in the caption rather than on the line. The markers are evenly spaced, and the slide says the gaps are not to scale.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | VSA zero-days | T1190
step: 02 | Execution | Encryptor as an update | T1072
step: 03 | Impact | Three levels of victim | T1486
step: 04 | The Uncomfortable Detail | Already reported | -
step: 05 | Resolution | A $70M demand | T1657
Arrows follow the page's stage order, not time: stage 04's report came before the attack.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's five stages, each with its phase and its ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, Initial Access, is the exploitation of zero-day flaws, including CVE-2021-30116, in internet-facing VSA servers. Stage 02, Execution, is the encryptor pushed through VSA's own agent update mechanism. Stage 03, Impact, is the encryption downstream: roughly 1,500 businesses through around 50 to 60 MSPs, and Coop Sweden's closed stores. Stage 04, under the page's heading The Uncomfortable Detail, carries no ATT&CK technique: DIVD had already reported the flaws, and a patch was in development. Stage 05, Resolution, is the $70 million demand and the decryptor that arrived anyway.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order, not as time. DIVD's report in stage 04 came before the attack in stage 01. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== REvil affiliates chained zero-days in on-premises, internet-facing VSA servers
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1190 | Exploit Public-Facing Application
fact: Flaws | authentication bypass and credential disclosure, including CVE-2021-30116
fact: Affected versions | before 9.5.7
fact: Timing | July 2, the Friday before the July 4 weekend
The server is reachable and authoritative over thousands of machines, and compromising it requires no lateral movement at all.
notes:
Stage 01 is the way in. The page maps it to T1190, Exploit Public-Facing Application.

REvil affiliates chained authentication bypass and credential disclosure flaws against on-premises Kaseya VSA servers exposed to the internet. Among them was CVE-2021-30116, which allowed credential disclosure via the default download page and authentication bypass on versions before 9.5.7. CVE stands for Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures, the standard list of vulnerability IDs. The page calls these zero-day vulnerabilities: flaws exploited before a fix was available.

VSA servers are exposed by design. MSP technicians need to reach them, and so do the agents on every managed endpoint. The page calls the management server a uniquely attractive target: it is reachable, it is authoritative over thousands of machines, and compromising it requires no lateral movement at all. Lateral movement means pivoting from one foothold to other systems.

The timing was chosen deliberately: the Friday before the July 4 weekend, when the people who would notice were least likely to be at a keyboard. The page says that pattern is worth planning around rather than being surprised by.

The next slide explains T1190 in general terms.

== What ATT&CK means by exploiting a public-facing application
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1190 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1190/
ATT&CK describes adversaries exploiting a weakness in an Internet-facing host or system to get initial access to a network. The weakness can be a software bug, a temporary glitch or a misconfiguration.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK's T1190, Exploit Public-Facing Application, sits under the Initial Access tactic. Adversaries may attempt to exploit a weakness in an Internet-facing host or system to initially access a network. The weakness can be a software bug, a temporary glitch or a misconfiguration.

ATT&CK says exploited applications are often websites or web servers, but can also include databases, standard services such as SMB or SSH, network device administration and management protocols, and any other system with Internet-accessible open sockets.

It adds that adversaries may also exploit edge network infrastructure and related appliances, specifically targeting devices that do not support robust host-based defenses.

== The VSA update channel delivered the encryptor to every managed endpoint
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Execution
technique: T1072 | Software Deployment Tools
technique: T1685 | Disable or Modify Tools
fact: Mechanism | the VSA agent's own update and script-deployment capability
fact: Privilege | whatever the RMM agent runs as, typically full administrative rights
fact: Alongside | steps to hinder endpoint protection during deployment
RMM software exists to run arbitrary code on managed machines with high privilege. An attacker holding the server inherits that.
notes:
With the VSA server under control, the actors used its legitimate agent update mechanism to distribute the ransomware encryptor to every managed endpoint. The page maps this to T1072, Software Deployment Tools, and T1685, Disable or Modify Tools.

RMM stands for remote monitoring and management: software that managed service providers use to administer their clients' estates. The page's point is that nothing had to be smuggled past anything. RMM software exists to run arbitrary code on managed machines with high privilege. That is the product, so an attacker holding the server inherits precisely that capability, delivered through the channel every endpoint is configured to trust.

The payload arrived with the trust properties of legitimate management traffic, and the deployment included steps to hinder endpoint protection. The agent runs with whatever privilege it is given, typically full administrative rights.

The page reads this next to the 3CX kill chain, and that comes back after the stages. Next, a diagram of the delivery path, then ATT&CK's general description of T1072.

== How the encryptor reached client machines, as the page describes it
layout: layers
layer: An MSP's VSA server | On-premises, internet-facing | Exploited by REvil affiliates
layer: VSA agent | On every managed endpoint | Typically full administrative rights
layer: Client machines | The encryptor arrives as a trusted update
Arrows show the delivery path the page describes. The endpoint saw a trusted management action from the expected server.
notes:
This slide draws the delivery path the page describes, from the attackers to client machines. Read the arrows as that path and nothing more.

REvil affiliates exploited on-premises VSA servers, internet-facing by design, through flaws including CVE-2021-30116. MSPs use those servers to administer their clients' estates. From a compromised server, the actors used the agent update mechanism to reach the VSA agent on every managed endpoint, and that agent typically runs with full administrative rights. The encryptor arrived on client machines as a trusted update from the company that manages their IT.

Compromising one VSA server reaches every client that provider administers. That is why, the page says, 50 to 60 compromises produced roughly 1,500 encrypted businesses.

The page does not describe the encryptor itself, or how endpoint protection was hindered, so the diagram does not either.

== What ATT&CK's T1072 technique covers
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1072 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1072/
ATT&CK describes adversaries using centralized software suites, such as configuration management and software deployment applications, to execute commands. It says access to network-wide endpoint management software may enable remote code execution on all connected systems.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK's T1072, Software Deployment Tools, covers adversaries who gain access to and use centralized software suites installed within an enterprise. Configuration management and software deployment applications may be used in an enterprise network or cloud environment for routine administration. ATT&CK's examples include SCCM, AWS Systems Manager, Microsoft Intune and Azure Arc.

It says access to network-wide or enterprise-wide endpoint management software may enable an adversary to achieve remote code execution on all connected systems. ATT&CK also lists moving laterally among the uses of such suites; the page says this attack needed no lateral movement.

The permissions required vary by system configuration. Local credentials may be enough with direct access to the third-party system, or specific domain credentials may be required, and the system may require an administrative account to log in or to access specific functionality.

== Roughly 50 to 60 MSPs compromised, about 1,500 businesses encrypted
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Impact
technique: T1486 | Data Encrypted for Impact
fact: Leverage | one management server reaches every client that provider administers
fact: Coop Sweden | around 800 stores closed; no relationship with Kaseya
fact: Worst affected | small businesses whose only IT capability was the compromised provider
The page calls amplification the whole point of targeting an MSP.
notes:
The page calls the amplification the whole point of targeting an MSP. Compromising one VSA server reaches every client that provider administers, so 50 to 60 compromises produced roughly 1,500 encrypted businesses. The page maps this stage to T1486, Data Encrypted for Impact, and calls it a leverage ratio no direct campaign achieves.

Coop Sweden is the page's clearest illustration of how far the blast travels. The grocery chain had no relationship with Kaseya at all, but its point-of-sale system was administered by an affected provider, and around 800 stores could not take payment and closed.

Small businesses were hit hardest, the page says, because the entire reason they use an MSP is that they have no internal IT capability. The compromise arrived through their only source of help, and removed it at the same moment.

The next slide sets out the three levels of victim in the page's own labels.

== One compromise, three levels of victim
layout: table
row: The page's label | Who | How many
row: Directly compromised | Managed service providers | ~50-60
row: Encrypted downstream | Businesses those providers administer | ~1,500
row: Third-order victim | Coop Sweden, with no relationship with Kaseya | ~800 stores closed
Coop's point-of-sale system was administered by an affected provider. The page calls Coop a downstream victim of a downstream victim.
notes:
These are the three levels of victim, in the labels the page uses for stage 03.

Around 50 to 60 managed service providers were directly compromised. About 1,500 businesses those providers administer were encrypted downstream. And Coop Sweden, the page's third-order victim, closed around 800 stores although it had no relationship with Kaseya at all.

Keep the hedges on all three counts: the page gives each one as approximate.

Coop's provider relationship comes back in the defenses, where the page sets out what to ask an MSP about its management plane.

== DIVD had already reported the flaws; a patch was in development
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: The Uncomfortable Detail
fact: Reported by | the Dutch Institute for Vulnerability Disclosure (DIVD)
fact: At the attack | patch in development
fact: Patch released | July 11-12, 2021, roughly ten days after the attack
fact: The general problem | the window between disclosure and deployment is real exposure
fact: In that window | compensating controls, not patience
The page says this was not a case of a defender who could not have known.
notes:
Stage 04 carries no ATT&CK technique. It is the detail the page calls uncomfortable.

The Dutch Institute for Vulnerability Disclosure had responsibly disclosed the vulnerabilities to Kaseya. DIVD researchers had found and reported the flaws, Kaseya was working on fixes, and the attack arrived inside the remediation window. The patch was released on July 11-12, 2021, roughly ten days after the attack.

The page calls this a hard problem rather than a simple failure. Disclosure timelines exist because patches take time to build and test, and there is no version of this where the vendor moves instantly.

But it makes one thing concrete for defenders. The gap between reported and patched everywhere is a period of real exposure for internet-facing management software. The mitigation available during it is compensating controls, such as taking the console off the public internet, restricting source addresses and monitoring it closely, rather than waiting.

The next slide explains responsible disclosure in general terms.

== What responsible, or coordinated, disclosure is
layout: background
source: OWASP Vulnerability Disclosure Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Vulnerability_Disclosure_Cheat_Sheet.html
OWASP calls responsible or coordinated disclosure a middle ground. The first report goes privately to the organization, and full details are published once a patch is available. Full disclosure publishes details as soon as they are found.
notes:
Background, from OWASP's Vulnerability Disclosure Cheat Sheet, in general terms.

OWASP describes three models. In private disclosure, the vulnerability is reported privately to the organization, which decides whether to publish the details. In full disclosure, the full details are made public as soon as they are identified, often before a patch is available. OWASP calls that approach very controversial, and says it should generally be a last resort.

Responsible or coordinated disclosure tries to find a reasonable middle ground between the two. The initial report is made privately, and the full details are published once a patch has been made available, sometimes with a delay to allow more time for the patches to be installed.

== A $70 million demand, and a decryptor that arrived anyway
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Resolution
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Demand | $70 million for a universal decryptor, not paid
fact: Decryptor | distributed by Kaseya later in July; the FBI obtained the key
fact: Contested | the delay between obtaining the key and releasing it to victims
fact: Recovery time | depended on whether the victim had tested backup restores
For most victims none of that mattered in the moment; their own backups did.
notes:
REvil demanded $70 million for a universal decryptor, and the page maps this stage to T1657, Financial Theft. It calls the demand a pricing decision as much as an extortion one: at that scale, negotiating individually with 1,500 businesses is operationally impossible, so the crew offered a single transaction instead. It was not paid.

The FBI obtained the decryption key, and Kaseya was able to distribute a universal decryptor to affected customers later in July. The FBI's decision to hold the key for a period while pursuing the actors, rather than releasing it immediately, drew sustained criticism from victims still down. REvil's infrastructure went offline days after the attack, and the group was subsequently disrupted.

For most victims none of that mattered in the moment. Recovery depended on their own backups, and the businesses that recovered fastest were the ones that had tested restoring from them.

The next slide explains the backup terms the page's defenses use.

== What 3-2-1, immutable and air-gapped backups mean
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, 3-2-1-1-0 Rule | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-three-two-one
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entries Immutable Backup and Air Gap (Cloud)
The classic 3-2-1 rule: three copies of data, on two distinct media or platforms, one offsite. An immutable backup cannot be modified or deleted before its retention window expires. An air gap stops an attacker on one network reaching another.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

The glossary describes the classic 3-2-1 backup rule as three copies of data, on two distinct media or platforms, with one copy offsite. Its extended form, 3-2-1-1-0, adds one immutable copy and zero errors when restoration is tested.

An immutable backup is backup data that cannot be modified or deleted before its retention window expires: not by accident, not by an attacker, not by an admin.

An air gap is physical or logical separation that prevents an attacker on one network from reaching another. The glossary notes that in cloud a true physical air gap is rare.

Also from the glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-immutable-backup and https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-air-gap

== When the distribution channel is compromised, the receiver has no signal
layout: text
The page reads this attack next to 3CX: a compromised distribution channel leaves the receiver no signal.
- The endpoint saw a trusted management action from the expected server.
- One VSA server reaches every client that provider administers.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws, and it reads this incident next to another kill chain on the site.

Read next to 3CX, the page says, the shared property is stark. When the distribution channel itself is compromised, the receiving end has no signal to work with, because everything about the delivery is exactly as it should be. Here the endpoint saw a trusted management action from the expected server.

Add the leverage. One management server reaches every client that provider administers, and the agent on each managed machine runs with whatever privilege the RMM agent has, typically full administrative rights.

That is why the page asks you to treat any management plane that can execute code on thousands of endpoints as the highest-value asset you operate. The defenses start there.

== Six defenses against five stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05
row: Management consoles off the public internet | 01
row: Ask your MSP about its management plane | 03
row: Limit and monitor the management agent | 02
row: Immutable, offline backups, with the restore rehearsed | 05
row: Plan for holiday and weekend timing | 01 | 02
row: Compensating controls for the disclosure-to-patch window | 04
A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something that stage has.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the five stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something that stage has.

Taking consoles off the public internet answers stage 01: the page says the exploited servers were reachable because that felt necessary. Asking your MSP about its management plane answers stage 03, because, the page says, Coop's exposure came from a provider relationship it had presumably never examined this way. Limiting and monitoring the management agent answers stage 02: it names endpoint-protection tampering initiated by the management agent, and mass script deployment.

Backups answer stage 05, where recovery time depended on tested restores and the decryptor arrived weeks later. Planning for holiday timing answers stage 01, launched into the Independence Day weekend on purpose, and stage 02, because one emergency action it names is disabling agent deployment. Compensating controls answer stage 04: the flaws were reported and a patch was in progress when the attack landed.

Every stage has at least one defense the page ties to it.

== Take consoles off the internet, and ask your MSP
layout: compare
col: Off the internet (stage 01) | Put consoles behind a VPN or identity-aware proxy | Restrict agent check-in to known source ranges, where supported | Treat any management plane that can execute code on thousands of endpoints as highest-value
col: Ask your MSP (stage 03) | Is the console internet-facing? How fast is it patched? | Is administrative access phishing-resistant and MFA-protected? | Does the contract say they will tell you promptly?
Coop had presumably never examined its provider relationship this way.
notes:
Two defenses about the management plane itself.

First, take RMM and management consoles off the public internet. The page says the exploited servers were reachable because that felt necessary. Put VSA-class consoles behind a VPN or an identity-aware proxy, which checks who is asking before a request reaches the application. Restrict agent check-in to known source ranges where the product supports it, and treat any management plane that can execute code on thousands of endpoints as the highest-value asset you operate.

Second, if you use an MSP, ask specifically about their management plane. Their RMM server is a direct execution path into your estate, so the questions are narrow: is the console internet-facing, how fast do they patch it, is administrative access phishing-resistant and protected by multi-factor authentication, and how would they notify you. Then make sure the contract says they will tell you promptly. Keep the page's word presumably: it says Coop had presumably never examined its provider relationship this way.

== Limit the management agent, and monitor it as an attack path
layout: compare
col: Limit what it can do (stage 02) | An agent running as SYSTEM everywhere is a pre-installed backdoor if the server falls | Scope agent privileges where the product allows
col: Monitor it (stage 02) | Alert on mass script deployment and unusual out-of-hours deployment jobs | Watch for endpoint-protection tampering initiated by the management agent
The page calls that combination close to definitive.
notes:
This defense answers stage 02, where the actors used the server's own agent update mechanism to distribute the encryptor, and the deployment included steps to hinder endpoint protection.

Limit what the management agent can do, and monitor it as an attack path. An RMM agent running as SYSTEM everywhere is a pre-installed backdoor if the server falls. SYSTEM is the most privileged local account on Windows. Scope agent privileges where the product allows.

Then alert on mass script deployment and unusual out-of-hours deployment jobs, and specifically watch for endpoint-protection tampering initiated by the management agent. The page says that combination is close to definitive.

== Plan for holiday and weekend timing
layout: text
The attack was launched into the Independence Day weekend on purpose. The page says that choice is now standard.
- Ensure on-call coverage over long weekends.
- Pre-authorize isolating a management server and disabling agent deployment.
- Rehearse it at least once.
notes:
This defense answers stage 01, which was timed for the US Independence Day weekend, and stage 02, because one emergency action it names is disabling agent deployment.

Plan for holiday and weekend timing explicitly. The attack was launched into the Independence Day weekend on purpose, and the page says that choice is now standard.

Ensure on-call coverage over long weekends. Pre-authorize the emergency actions responders will need, such as isolating a management server and disabling agent deployment, so nobody is hunting for approval on a public holiday. Then rehearse it at least once.

== Back up for this, and prepare for the window before a patch
layout: compare
col: Backups (stage 05) | Follow 3-2-1 with at least one immutable or air-gapped copy | Backup infrastructure not administered by the same RMM that could be compromised | Time an actual restore rather than assuming
col: Before the patch (stage 04) | Reduce exposure of management software in advance | Subscribe to vendor and CISA advisories for your critical-path products | Be able to take a console off the internet within the hour
Recovery time here was determined almost entirely by backup quality.
notes:
Two defenses for before and after.

First, keep immutable, offline backups and rehearse the restore. The page says recovery time here was determined almost entirely by backup quality, and the universal decryptor arrived weeks later for those still waiting. Follow 3-2-1 with at least one immutable or air-gapped copy, make sure backup infrastructure is not administered by the same RMM that could be compromised, and time an actual restore rather than assuming.

Second, have compensating controls ready for the disclosure-to-patch window. The flaws were reported and a patch was in progress when the attack landed, so, the page says, patching promptly would not have helped. What does help is reducing exposure of management software in advance, subscribing to vendor and CISA advisories for the products in your critical path, and being able to take a console off the internet within the hour when something is announced. CISA is the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Is any RMM or management console reachable from the public internet?
check: Does your MSP's console face the internet, and how fast does it patch?
check: Would mass script deployment or out-of-hours deployment jobs raise an alert?
check: Would endpoint-protection tampering by the management agent raise an alert?
check: Is at least one backup copy immutable or air-gapped? Have you timed a restore?
check: Are isolating a management server and disabling agent deployment pre-authorized?
check: Could you take a console off the internet within the hour?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether any RMM or management console is reachable from the public internet, and whether agent check-in is restricted to known source ranges where the product supports it. If you use an MSP, ask whether its console is internet-facing, how fast it patches, and how it would notify you.

Ask whether anything would alert on mass script deployment, unusual out-of-hours deployment jobs, or endpoint-protection tampering initiated by the management agent.

Ask whether at least one backup copy is immutable or air-gapped, whether backup infrastructure is administered by the same RMM that could be compromised, and whether you have timed a restore. Ask whether isolating a management server and disabling agent deployment are pre-authorized for a public holiday. Finally, ask whether you could take a console off the internet within the hour.

== Which of your systems could push code to every endpoint?
layout: question
RMM software exists to run arbitrary code on managed machines with high privilege. An attacker holding the server inherits that capability.

Name one system, yours or a provider's, that can execute code on thousands of your endpoints. Is it internet-facing?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page's point about RMM is that the capability is the product. Software that exists to run arbitrary code on managed machines with high privilege hands that capability to whoever holds the server, through the channel every endpoint trusts.

Ask each person to name one specific system, their own or their provider's, before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says to do about it.

== What the page says about management consoles
layout: answer
- Put management consoles behind a VPN or identity-aware proxy, off the public internet.
- Restrict agent check-in to known source ranges where the product supports it.
- If an MSP runs it, ask about its console, patching and notification.
notes:
The page's answer starts with exposure. Take RMM and management consoles off the public internet: put VSA-class consoles behind a VPN or an identity-aware proxy, and restrict agent check-in to known source ranges where the product supports it.

If a provider runs the console, the page says to ask narrow questions: is the console internet-facing, how fast do they patch it, is administrative access phishing-resistant and MFA-protected, and how would they notify you. Then get prompt notification into the contract.

Bring the group's examples back to the page's framing. Any management plane that can execute code on thousands of endpoints is, the page says, the highest-value asset you operate.

== What could you do between reported and patched?
layout: question
DIVD had reported the flaws, and a patch was in development, when the attack landed. The patch came roughly ten days later.

If a flaw in your console were announced today, what would you do before the patch?
notes:
This question comes from stage 04.

The page is plain that this was not a case of a defender who could not have known. DIVD had reported the flaws, Kaseya was working on fixes, and the attack arrived inside the remediation window. The patch was released on July 11-12, 2021, roughly ten days after the attack.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to say what it would change during those days.

== Compensating controls, not patience
layout: answer
Patching promptly would not have helped here, the page says.
- Reduce exposure of management software in advance.
- Subscribe to vendor and CISA advisories for your critical-path products.
- Be able to take a console off the internet within the hour.
notes:
The page's answer is compensating controls, ready before they are needed.

During the gap between reported and patched everywhere, the page lists taking the console off the public internet, restricting source addresses and monitoring it closely, rather than waiting. Its defense names three things that help: reduce the exposure of management software in advance, subscribe to vendor and CISA advisories for the products in your critical path, and be able to take a console off the internet within the hour when something is announced.

Bring the group's ideas back to this. The page calls the window a hard problem rather than a simple failure, because patches take time to build and test.

